{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794305537383,"sessionID":"ses_e3b11b142ffehyUsg0hi8c2GGs","part":{"id":"prt_1c4f1b551001T7flDtUIPt1H1v","messageID":"msg_1c4f18cf6001mJvnxrCTPDvM3k","sessionID":"ses_e3b11b142ffehyUsg0hi8c2GGs","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794305537384,"sessionID":"ses_e3b11b142ffehyUsg0hi8c2GGs","part":{"id":"prt_1c4f1b5530012w5KHpF0XiPru9","messageID":"msg_1c4f18cf6001mJvnxrCTPDvM3k","sessionID":"ses_e3b11b142ffehyUsg0hi8c2GGs","type":"text","text":"<think>All API tests pass. Now let me build the frontend.\n\nLet me set up Tailwind v4 and the React app.\n\nSteps:\n1. Move tailwindcss and @tailwindcss/vite to devDependencies in package.json, run npm install to update the lock.\n2. vite.config.ts: add the tailwind plugin.\n3. src/index.css with `@import \"tailwindcss\";`\n4. Build components.\n\nLet me first update package.json and vite config, then write the React app.\n\nFor the router, I'll write a small one. Let me create the files.\n\nFiles:\n- frontend/src/main.tsx\n- frontend/src/index.css\n- frontend/src/api.ts\n- frontend/src/router.tsx\n- frontend/src/components/... \n- frontend/src/pages/...\n- frontend/src/App.tsx\n\nLet me design the UI carefully with Tailwind.\n\nColor scheme: slate/indigo. Header: dark slate bar. Cards: white with border and rounded corners, shadow-sm.\n\nLet me write api.ts first.\n\n```ts\nexport interface Book {\n  id: number;\n  title: string;\n  author: string;\n  year: number | null;\n  isbn: string | null;\n  created_at: string;\n  review_count: number;\n  average_rating: number | null;\n}\n\nexport interface Review {\n  id: number;\n  book_id: number;\n  rating: number;\n  comment: string;\n  created_at: string;\n  username: string;\n}\n\nexport interface BookListResponse { items: Book[]; total: number; page: number; page_size: number; }\nexport interface ReviewListResponse { items: Review[]; }\nexport interface User { id: number; username: string; }\n```\n\nNow the App:\n\n```tsx\nexport default function App() {\n  const path = usePath();\n  const [token, setToken] = useState<string | null>(() => localStorage.getItem(TOKEN_KEY));\n  const [user, setUser] = useState<User | null>(null);\n  const [authChecked, setAuthChecked] = useState(false);\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nOn mount, if a token exists → call /api/auth/me → set user or clear token.\n\nAuth context: pass `user`, `token`, `login`, `register`, `logout` down via props. I'll create a simple context to avoid prop drilling: `AuthContext`.\n\nLet me write an `AuthProvider` in `src/auth.tsx` with a context hook `useAuth()`.\n\nRoutes parsing in App:\n```tsx\nconst path = usePath();\nconst match = /^\\/books\\/(\\d+)(\\/edit)?$/.exec(path);\n```\n\nLet me write the code.\n\nFor the search box: debounce with useEffect + setTimeout, updating a `query` state used for fetching. I'll keep `searchInput` state and a `query` state updated after 300ms.\n\nPaging: page state; when query changes, reset page to 1.\n\nDelete confirmation: `window.confirm(\"Delete this book and its reviews?\")`.\n\nLet me write the components now.\n\nI'll write:\n- src/index.css\n- src/api.ts\n- src/auth.tsx\n- src/router.tsx\n- src/components/Header.tsx\n- src/components/AuthPanel.tsx\n- src/components/Field.tsx (input with label + error)\n- src/components/Pagination.tsx\n- src/components/Rating.tsx (stars display)\n- src/pages/BooksPage.tsx\n- src/pages/BookPage.tsx\n- src/pages/BookFormPage.tsx\n- src/App.tsx\n- src/main.tsx\n\nLet me write these.\n\napi.ts details — handle 204 responses.\n\n```ts\nconst TOKEN_KEY = \"booklib.token\";\n\nexport class ApiError extends Error {\n  status: number;\n  errors: Record<string, string>;\n  constructor(status: number, message: string, errors: Record<string, string> = {}) {\n    super(message);\n    this.name = \"ApiError\";\n    this.status = status;\n    this.errors = errors;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nNote: extending Error with TS target ES5 breaks `instanceof`. Vite's default target for build is 'modules' (esnext-ish, browserslist). To be safe use `class ApiError extends Error` with `Object.setPrototypeOf(this, ApiError.prototype)`. Or target ES2020 in tsconfig. Let me check the tsconfig files in frontend — there were none listed in the file list! Only package.json, index.html, vite.config.ts, src/main.tsx, src/App.tsx, package-lock.json. There's no tsconfig.json! The Vite React plugin works without tsconfig but TS won't be type-checked; esbuild handles .tsx.\n\nI should add a tsconfig.json for proper TypeScript support (the task says TypeScript). Let me add tsconfig.json with sensible settings, and also add a \"typecheck\" script using `tsc --noEmit` — that requires typescript as a devDependency. Let me add typescript and @types/react, @types/react-dom as devDependencies. That's good practice for a TS project and lets me verify types compile.\n\nNote: `npm run build` in the Docker image runs `vite build` only, which doesn't typecheck (esbuild transpiles). If I add `tsc -b && vite build`, a type error would break the Docker build. Safer: keep `build` as `vite build` and add a separate `typecheck` script.\n\nHmm, but should the build include typechecking? It's nice, but risky if the environment differs. I'll keep build = vite build and add \"typecheck\": \"tsc --noEmit\".\n\nLet me install typescript, @types/react, @types/react-dom as devDependencies.\n\nAlso, the tsconfig should have \"jsx\": \"react-jsx\", \"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\", \"strict\": true, \"noEmit\": true, \"isolatedModules\": true, \"verbatimModuleSyntax\": true, \"skipLibCheck\": true, \"include\": [\"src\"].\n\nWith verbatimModuleSyntax, type-only imports must use `import type`. I'll write code accordingly.\n\nLet me now install the deps.</think>\n\nNow let me set up the frontend with TypeScript and Tailwind:","time":{"start":1794305537363,"end":1794305537379}}}
